 ('nl-competitors-intent', 'Analyze the Competition', 'intent',
  $q$Record each named competitor and do-it-yourself workaround, where the platform wins and loses, and how long each win would take them to copy.$q$,
  $q$Your task_context carries the competitors to cover (or none, to choose them), the existing competitor, customer_profile and correction records, and the shipped features. Steps: (1) choose or confirm the competitors: named products and the do-it-yourself workarounds the first customer uses instead — keep every one the corrections say to keep, never re-add one they cut; (2) with web search, read each one's site, pricing page and documentation, and at least one review or analyst write-up, recording source and date; (3) for each: what it does, published pricing, where it wins, and where the platform wins, each point cited; (4) run the copy test on every platform win against that competitor — why they do not do it today, the evidence, the moat type, the time to copy in months, the verdict; (5) update an existing competitor record rather than duplicating it; (6) return records_to_write: one competitor record per competitor, data.copy_tests for every platform win, status draft. Every benefit or differentiator carries its copy_test (THE COPY TEST in the knowledge Skill): the competitor, why they do not do it today with evidence, the moat type, the time to copy in months, and the verdict; a feature moat under six months is weak, and a weak item never leads. Napkin entries you are given rank above web research (THE NAPKIN RULE): cite them [napkin:<entry id>], list each one you used in napkin_notes with a single line saying what you did with it and which record it became, and list the rest in napkin_left untouched.$q$,
