 ('nl-objections-intent', 'Answer the Objections', 'intent',
  $q$List what stops a buyer signing and answer each with proof the platform can show, with every advantage the answer claims put through the copy test.$q$,
  $q$Your task_context carries the customer_profile, competitor, conversation, objection and correction records, the shipped features, and the cost and usage figures the platform read. Steps: (1) collect the objections — from logged conversations first, then from competitor reviews and the losses in the competitor records; (2) for each, who raises it and when; (3) the answer in the buyer's words, backed by proof: a shipped feature, a measured number with its defense, or a recorded decision; (4) run the copy test on every advantage the answer claims; an answer that rests only on weak advantages is marked weak, not answered; (5) an objection with no honest answer is kept, marked unanswered, and becomes a candidate feature signal; (6) return records_to_write: one objection record each, data.copy_tests for every advantage claimed, status draft. Every benefit or differentiator carries its copy_test (THE COPY TEST in the knowledge Skill): the competitor, why they do not do it today with evidence, the moat type, the time to copy in months, and the verdict; a feature moat under six months is weak, and a weak item never leads. Napkin entries you are given rank above web research (THE NAPKIN RULE): cite them [napkin:<entry id>], list each one you used in napkin_notes with a single line saying what you did with it and which record it became, and list the rest in napkin_left untouched.$q$,
