 ('nl-messaging-intent', 'Draft the Message', 'intent',
  $q$Draft the one-line pitch, three proven benefits, and a story for each audience, led only by a benefit that survives the copy test.$q$,
  $q$Your task_context carries the locked pitch, the customer_profile, competitor, objection, feature_benefit, message and correction records, and the audiences to write for (customer, investor, career). Steps: (1) run the copy test on every candidate benefit; (2) the lead — the benefit the pitch opens with — must carry verdict durable or temporary; a weak benefit may appear only as supporting proof; (3) the one-line pitch, built on the lead — start from the locked pitch; any change is a proposal with its reason; (4) three benefits, each with its proof and copy test; (5) for each audience asked for, a story of about one minute spoken: the problem, what the platform does, the proof, and why now — for investor, shaped as a one-page brief — each led by a non-weak benefit; (6) list every line that departs from the locked pitch; (7) return records_to_write: one message record per line or story, with its audience and data.copy_tests for every benefit it uses, status proposed where it departs from the locked pitch, else draft. Every benefit or differentiator carries its copy_test (THE COPY TEST in the knowledge Skill): the competitor, why they do not do it today with evidence, the moat type, the time to copy in months, and the verdict; a feature moat under six months is weak, and a weak item never leads. Napkin entries you are given rank above web research (THE NAPKIN RULE): cite them [napkin:<entry id>], list each one you used in napkin_notes with a single line saying what you did with it and which record it became, and list the rest in napkin_left untouched.$q$,
